SORT(1)                        P32W Unix                         SORT(1)

NAME
       sort - sort lines of text

SYNOPSIS
       sort [-rnu] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       sort reads the lines of the files, or of the standard input if
       no file is named (or for a file named -), sorts them all
       together and writes them to the standard output. By default
       the order is that of the character codes (ASCII), comparing
       whole lines. Lines may be of any length; a last line without
       a newline is given one.

OPTIONS
       Options may be combined (-rn) or given separately; -- ends
       them. An unknown option is a usage error.

       -n     numeric: compare the integer at the start of each line
              (after any blanks, with an optional sign); lines with
              equal numbers, or no number (taken as 0), are then
              compared as text. Integers of any size compare
              correctly.
       -r     reverse the order
       -u     unique: write only one of each set of identical lines
              (whole lines; with -n, 7 and 07 are both kept)

EXAMPLES
       sort names > names.sorted
       du /usr | sort -rn | head     the biggest directories
       sort -r /etc/passwd

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error or if a file could not be opened
       (sort says why and sorts the others).

SEE ALSO
       uniq(1), grep(1), du(1)

BUGS
       There are no keys or fields (-k, -t, +pos), and no -f, -b,
       -d, -m, -c or -o.

       -n understands integers only, not fractions.

       The whole input is held in memory, so the largest file that
       can be sorted is limited by the process's memory.
